# Vendored: planner (the tracking companion's app)

Pinned snapshot of the node's work tracker: the six-stage task pipeline, the
objective hierarchy, projects, and the worklog/plan references.
`companions/tracking.sh` installs this directory as a native service
(`smart-node-planner.service`); no image is built or loaded any more.

Upstream:  private first-party repository
Tag:       v0.4.0
Commit:    49882c0  (a trusted key may name the caller it acts for)
Vendored:  2026-08-13

Why it is vendored at all: it was the ONE component the installer did not carry,
so `tools/build-images.sh` required `--planner-repo /path/to/a/private/checkout`.
Anyone without access to that repo could not build the planner image, the
tracking companion had nothing to load, and the install died there -- i.e. the
repo could not be handed to anyone outside the team, however open the rest of it
was. Vendoring it matches every other component (doci, mesh, brin, gateway,
core-api ...) and makes this repo the whole input again.

Why v0.3.1 and not v0.3.0: v0.3.0 could not produce a working container. Its
`pyproject.toml` had `httpx` only in the `dev` extra while `planner/notify.py`
imports it at module level, so an image built the documented way (`pip install .`)
died at import with "No module named 'httpx'" and never became healthy. The image
that had been running on nodes was built from some other, unrecorded state, so the
break stayed invisible until this vendoring rebuilt from the tag -- and then a
clean install failed at the tracking companion. Fixed upstream in v0.3.1, which
also aligns VERSION / `__version__` / tag on 0.3.1 (they had said 0.2.0 / 0.3.0 /
v0.3.0).

Why v0.4.0: the node needed a credential on the planner without losing
who each entry belongs to. `api_keys.can_delegate` lets ONE holder -- core-api,
the door that resolves callers -- present a credential and name the real author
in the same request, while every other key ignores the name header entirely.
Before this the node ran the planner in `dev` mode on a bridge address anything
could reach, so every entry was authored `dev` and the owner's approval never
matched what brin releases on.

Re-vendoring: `git -C <planner checkout> archive v<version> | tar -x -C
companions/planner`, then update this file and `tracking.sh` STAGE_VERSION (it
names both the image tag and the tarball). Diff before you overwrite -- vendored
trees on this project have drifted in BOTH directions.

Do NOT use `tools/revendor.sh` for this tree as it stands: its rsync `--delete`
removes this VENDORED file (nothing excludes it) and it copies `.pytest_cache/`
in from a checkout that has run its tests. `git archive` from the tag carries
tracked files only, which is why the recipe above is the one written down.
